Yes. The main problem is that your current test is starting a full Spring Boot context just to test SendFlexWebClientService. That is unnecessary here and can make the test suite appear stuck, especially with MockWebServer, WebClient configuration, and retry handling.

I recommend replacing the entire test class with a pure unit/integration-style test using a manually created WebClient and MockWebServer. This removes:

@SpringBootTest

HttpClientAutoConfig

LoggingConfig

Spring's webClientNoAuth bean

Spring context startup/shutdown

Mockito Spring integration


It will be much faster and won't get stuck starting the application context.

Replace the entire class with this

