Why this version should not get stuck

The important change is that I removed this entire block:

@SpringBootTest(
    classes = {
        HttpClientAutoConfig.class,
        LoggingConfig.class,
        SendFlexWebClientService.class,
        AdvShippingIntegrationApiProperties.class,
        ObjectMapper.class,
        RetryConfig.class
    })

and also removed:

@Autowired
private ...

Instead, the test creates the service directly:

subject =
    new SendFlexWebClientService(
        retryConfig,
        webClient,
        properties,
        perfTestDeterminer);

So there is no Spring application startup.

Your log showed:

Started SendFlexWebClientServiceMockServerTest in 14.289 seconds

That's already a strong indication that the full Spring context was contributing unnecessary overhead.


---

One more important point

I deliberately did not add a test that causes a 5xx response.

Your production code has:

.retryWhen(
    retryConfig.createRetry(
        MAX_ATTEMPTS,
        INITIAL_DELAY_SECONDS))

A real 5xx test can wait through the configured retry backoff and make the test suite look frozen. Since your current objective is to get this test class executing reliably, don't introduce that yet.

Once this class runs successfully, we can add the retry-specific tests separately using a mocked RetryConfig, so they don't actually wait for retry delays.

Also, if your production code is now:

.body(
    requestBody == null
        ? BodyInserters.empty()
        : BodyInserters.fromValue(requestBody))

we can add the null-body branch afterward without bringing back the hanging behavior.